WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:02.820
- He's an American entrepreneur,
venture capitalist,

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:04.224
and hedge fund manager.

00:00:04.224 --> 00:00:07.424
- He co-founded PayPal, with
Max Levchin and Elon Musk.

00:00:07.424 --> 00:00:10.795
- He's ranked fourth on the
Forbs Midas list of 2014,

00:00:10.795 --> 00:00:12.490
at 2.2 billion dollars.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:14.491
- He's Peter Thiel and here

00:00:14.491 --> 00:00:17.129
are his top ten rules for success.

00:00:17.129 --> 00:00:20.277
(swooshing)

00:00:20.277 --> 00:00:22.505
- You are the entrepreneur of your life.

00:00:22.505 --> 00:00:25.873
And you can, you can decide you know,

00:00:25.873 --> 00:00:28.502
what you're going to do, what
you're going to prioritize,

00:00:28.502 --> 00:00:30.980
and you should never forget that you have

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.915
a tremendous amount of freedom as it is

00:00:32.915 --> 00:00:34.680
to make these very basic decisions

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.512
on what you're going to do with your life.

00:00:37.512 --> 00:00:40.051
You can start anytime you want.

00:00:40.051 --> 00:00:41.958
The most critical thing for every start up

00:00:41.958 --> 00:00:45.208
is to be doing one thing uniquely well,

00:00:46.089 --> 00:00:47.844
better than anyone else in the world.

00:00:47.844 --> 00:00:51.580
Technology's a fundamentally
global business

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:55.607
and the really great technology companies,

00:00:55.607 --> 00:00:57.844
are doing something significantly better

00:00:57.844 --> 00:01:00.939
than anybody else in the
world and you want to be

00:01:00.939 --> 00:01:02.600
in that sort of position.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.716
I think if you're just
starting a business,

00:01:04.716 --> 00:01:07.177
one of the questions that's
always valuable to answer

00:01:07.177 --> 00:01:09.724
is what do you know
that's true that nobody

00:01:09.724 --> 00:01:12.807
else understands or more prosaically,

00:01:13.881 --> 00:01:17.630
what great business exists
that nobody is building?

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:20.963
One part that I'm always very focused on

00:01:21.832 --> 00:01:24.547
is sort of, the structure
of these companies

00:01:24.547 --> 00:01:27.166
and how well are people aligned?

00:01:27.166 --> 00:01:31.414
So you can and making sure
that people are you know

00:01:31.414 --> 00:01:33.881
aligned properly, not just
that they say they are.

00:01:33.881 --> 00:01:37.126
I think this is sort of a
very, a very legal kind of a

00:01:37.126 --> 00:01:38.556
framework to think about;

00:01:38.556 --> 00:01:40.482
what are peoples' incentives, you know,

00:01:40.482 --> 00:01:42.119
are they going to be aligned or not

00:01:42.119 --> 00:01:44.875
and I think you want to
both have formal alignment,

00:01:44.875 --> 00:01:46.425
where you set the structure right,

00:01:46.425 --> 00:01:49.027
and you want to have informal alignment,

00:01:49.027 --> 00:01:52.214
where the people would naturally
try to work together well

00:01:52.214 --> 00:01:54.047
in one way or another.

00:01:55.075 --> 00:01:57.553
And I think that somehow
tends to get obscured

00:01:57.553 --> 00:01:59.335
in all these other ways.

00:01:59.335 --> 00:02:03.077
You know there's always, there's
always a, there's always a

00:02:03.077 --> 00:02:07.737
pre-history question I like
to ask founders of companies

00:02:07.737 --> 00:02:11.320
which is how did you,
how did you um, meet,

00:02:13.238 --> 00:02:16.904
what's the pre-history of the
company before you got started

00:02:16.904 --> 00:02:19.494
and I find that to be a really
instructive set of questions

00:02:19.494 --> 00:02:22.686
to ask; a bad answer is
something like, you know,

00:02:22.686 --> 00:02:26.547
we met a week ago at a
social networking function

00:02:26.547 --> 00:02:27.888
at a business networking function.

00:02:27.888 --> 00:02:29.200
We decided to start a
company because we both

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.348
wanted to be entrepreneurs.

00:02:31.348 --> 00:02:32.451
You know that's like saying,

00:02:32.451 --> 00:02:34.453
I married the first person
I met at the slot machines

00:02:34.453 --> 00:02:37.038
in Las Vegas and you
might hit the jackpot,

00:02:37.038 --> 00:02:39.209
but it's probably a really bad idea.

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:41.413
A much better answer is you
know, we've been talking

00:02:41.413 --> 00:02:43.088
about his for a long time.

00:02:43.088 --> 00:02:45.904
You know, maybe one of us
is more on the tech side,

00:02:45.904 --> 00:02:47.608
the other ones more on the business side.

00:02:47.608 --> 00:02:49.979
We have these sort of complimentary skills

00:02:49.979 --> 00:02:54.021
and so I think this question
of alignment and structure

00:02:54.021 --> 00:02:56.601
is incredibly important.

00:02:56.601 --> 00:03:01.282
I think that all, if you're
a founder or entrepreneur,

00:03:01.282 --> 00:03:03.742
what you want to aim for is monopoly.

00:03:03.742 --> 00:03:07.255
You want to aim to build a
company that is one of a kind.

00:03:07.255 --> 00:03:11.687
That's so far differentiated
from the competition

00:03:11.687 --> 00:03:14.807
that it's not even competing.

00:03:14.807 --> 00:03:17.611
And I this conventionalism
is always with capitals

00:03:17.611 --> 00:03:20.288
and competition are somehow synonyms;

00:03:20.288 --> 00:03:21.524
I believe they're antonyms.

00:03:21.524 --> 00:03:23.598
A capitalist is someone
whose in the business of

00:03:23.598 --> 00:03:26.816
accumulating capital; a
world of perfect competition

00:03:26.816 --> 00:03:30.019
is a world where all the
profits are competed away.

00:03:30.019 --> 00:03:33.020
If you want to compete like
crazy then you should just

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:35.437
open a restaurant in Chicago.

00:03:37.186 --> 00:03:41.576
And um, and I think the
great companies like Google,

00:03:41.576 --> 00:03:43.457
so the paradigm example I use,

00:03:43.457 --> 00:03:46.972
has had no serious competition
in Search since 2002

00:03:46.972 --> 00:03:48.765
when it definitively distance itself

00:03:48.765 --> 00:03:51.454
from Yahoo and Microsoft
and as a result it's been

00:03:51.454 --> 00:03:55.265
generating enormous cash
flows for the last 12 years.

00:03:55.265 --> 00:03:59.314
But yeah, there probably are
a lot of people who end up

00:03:59.314 --> 00:04:02.670
trying to be somewhat fake entrepreneurs

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:05.923
where the goal is to be an entrepreneur.

00:04:05.923 --> 00:04:07.534
If you ask people what do you
want to do with your life?

00:04:07.534 --> 00:04:08.367
What do you want to do when you grow up?

00:04:08.367 --> 00:04:09.839
I want to be an entrepreneur,

00:04:09.839 --> 00:04:13.200
which I always think is
a somewhat too common,

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.369
disturbingly somewhat too common

00:04:15.369 --> 00:04:18.884
and I think you know it's
a, it's saying that you

00:04:18.884 --> 00:04:20.659
want to be entrepreneur
sort of like saying

00:04:20.659 --> 00:04:22.750
I want to be rich or I want to be famous.

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:25.775
You know, nobody in their
right mind starts a company

00:04:25.775 --> 00:04:27.545
for the sake of starting a company.

00:04:27.545 --> 00:04:29.732
You start a company because
there's a very important

00:04:29.732 --> 00:04:32.357
problem to solve that's not getting solved

00:04:32.357 --> 00:04:34.771
in large you know,
governmental or nonprofit

00:04:34.771 --> 00:04:37.685
or for profit institutions and that's why

00:04:37.685 --> 00:04:39.403
you actually need to start a new company.

00:04:39.403 --> 00:04:41.919
Just starting a company
for the sake of doing so

00:04:41.919 --> 00:04:44.306
is a really odd thing to do.

00:04:44.306 --> 00:04:46.857
On an individual level
I think it is always,

00:04:46.857 --> 00:04:49.799
it's always really good
if there's something

00:04:49.799 --> 00:04:52.121
that you're incredibly passionate about

00:04:52.121 --> 00:04:56.290
and just sort of are find to
be intrinsically interesting

00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:59.457
and the people pursue that and so the,

00:05:00.612 --> 00:05:04.445
you know one of the
resolutions I came up with

00:05:05.753 --> 00:05:08.930
a number of years ago was to always

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:13.097
value substance over status;
substance over prestige.

00:05:14.173 --> 00:05:17.929
And you know if I sort of was
giving my younger self advice

00:05:17.929 --> 00:05:22.096
on what to, what to do or how
to think about ones' life,

00:05:23.804 --> 00:05:28.023
I probably, I think I you
know, I probably would still

00:05:28.023 --> 00:05:31.940
go to Stanford, I might
still go to law school.

00:05:32.925 --> 00:05:36.197
But I'd ask sort of, I'd
ask a lot more questions

00:05:36.197 --> 00:05:39.419
why I was doing these things and I think

00:05:39.419 --> 00:05:42.023
I think if I was honest about it,

00:05:42.023 --> 00:05:46.610
too much of it was driven
by, by prestige and status

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:51.212
and not quite enough about
really the substance of

00:05:51.212 --> 00:05:52.363
trying to learn things.

00:05:52.363 --> 00:05:55.027
I have sort of this, I sort
of just think of it as this

00:05:55.027 --> 00:05:57.883
sort of crazy rolling quarter life crisis

00:05:57.883 --> 00:06:01.873
and sort of culminated in
this big New York law firm

00:06:01.873 --> 00:06:04.616
where you know, from the outside
everybody wanted to get in.

00:06:04.616 --> 00:06:07.346
From the inside everybody
wanted to get out.

00:06:07.346 --> 00:06:10.398
You know, after, I lasted
seven months and three days

00:06:10.398 --> 00:06:14.565
and after, when I left one of
the people down the hall said,

00:06:16.077 --> 00:06:17.802
it's so reassuring to see you leave Peter.

00:06:17.802 --> 00:06:20.579
I had no idea it was possible
to escape from Alcatraz.

00:06:20.579 --> 00:06:24.618
Which again, and again I,
and all you had to do was go

00:06:24.618 --> 00:06:27.092
through the front door, but our identity,

00:06:27.092 --> 00:06:30.302
people's identities get so wrapped up in

00:06:30.302 --> 00:06:33.420
the things they compete for
that it was inconceivable

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:35.215
for people to actually do that

00:06:35.215 --> 00:06:36.949
and then the question was you know,

00:06:36.949 --> 00:06:38.466
well how had I ended up there?

00:06:38.466 --> 00:06:40.880
Why had I not thought about that more?

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:44.016
And I think it was that I
had taken too many of these

00:06:44.016 --> 00:06:47.679
shortcuts of valuing sort
of what was prestigious,

00:06:47.679 --> 00:06:51.558
what was conventional over
what I really wanted to do.

00:06:51.558 --> 00:06:54.476
So I think always substance over status.

00:06:54.476 --> 00:06:57.017
And this is why I think
competitions always this very

00:06:57.017 --> 00:07:00.252
two edged thing; when
you compete ferociously,

00:07:00.252 --> 00:07:03.316
you will get better at that
which you are competing on,

00:07:03.316 --> 00:07:05.201
but you will always narrow your focus

00:07:05.201 --> 00:07:07.023
to beating the people around you

00:07:07.023 --> 00:07:09.959
and it often comes at
this very hight price

00:07:09.959 --> 00:07:13.042
of losing sight of what is more important

00:07:13.042 --> 00:07:14.915
or perhaps more valuable.

00:07:14.915 --> 00:07:19.309
The general theme I would
suggest is that all trends

00:07:19.309 --> 00:07:24.074
are overrated and so if you
think about current trends

00:07:24.074 --> 00:07:26.074
in technology, you know,

00:07:29.251 --> 00:07:33.418
health care, I.T. software,
education software, overrated.

00:07:34.378 --> 00:07:37.795
SAS enterprise software really overrated.

00:07:38.629 --> 00:07:42.341
Big data, cloud computing,
if you hear those words

00:07:42.341 --> 00:07:44.409
you need to think fraud;
you need to run away

00:07:44.409 --> 00:07:46.933
as fast as you possibly can.

00:07:46.933 --> 00:07:50.183
And the reason these buzzwords tell you

00:07:51.329 --> 00:07:55.110
that something, these buzzwords
are sort of like a tell

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:57.474
in poker that people are bluffing

00:07:57.474 --> 00:07:59.629
and that there's nothing,
that the business

00:07:59.629 --> 00:08:02.957
is not undifferentiated
because the buzzwords tell you

00:08:02.957 --> 00:08:07.377
that it is one company of a
category that's undifferentiated

00:08:07.377 --> 00:08:10.357
from the others in that
category and therefore,

00:08:10.357 --> 00:08:14.621
are symptomatic somehow
of a lot of competition

00:08:14.621 --> 00:08:16.907
and a bad business idea.

00:08:16.907 --> 00:08:20.037
And so you know, you don't
want to be the fourth online

00:08:20.037 --> 00:08:22.068
pet food company, you
don't want to be the tenth

00:08:22.068 --> 00:08:24.150
tin foam solar panel
company, you don't want to be

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:26.368
the one-thousandth
restaurant in San Francisco

00:08:26.368 --> 00:08:29.527
and so there's something
about if you can describe

00:08:29.527 --> 00:08:33.143
what a company is doing
very straight forwardly

00:08:33.143 --> 00:08:37.269
by referencing these
buzzwords, these categories

00:08:37.269 --> 00:08:38.862
that already exist.

00:08:38.862 --> 00:08:41.444
That's actually a sign
that it's a pretty bad,

00:08:41.444 --> 00:08:43.679
that it's a pretty bad idea.

00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:46.932
I think there's a problem
in western Europe where,

00:08:46.932 --> 00:08:51.504
where failure is, it's
too impossible to fail.

00:08:51.504 --> 00:08:54.332
Once you fail you can never,
you can never start over.

00:08:54.332 --> 00:08:57.385
I think there's an opposite
version of this in California

00:08:57.385 --> 00:09:00.974
where I think sometimes
people are a little bit too

00:09:00.974 --> 00:09:04.528
facile about failure because
I think it actually is always

00:09:04.528 --> 00:09:08.281
incredibly damaging to
people and if you work

00:09:08.281 --> 00:09:11.986
really hard at something
and it doesn't work

00:09:11.986 --> 00:09:15.947
that's, you know, that's
going to be psychologically

00:09:15.947 --> 00:09:19.004
damaging, you know, people
have less confidence.

00:09:19.004 --> 00:09:21.852
All sorts of reasons why that tends to be,

00:09:21.852 --> 00:09:23.569
that tends quite bad.

00:09:23.569 --> 00:09:27.859
I think that when people, I
don't think there's that much

00:09:27.859 --> 00:09:30.360
you learn from failure by the way because

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:33.085
it's typically overdetermined;
it's like why is technology

00:09:33.085 --> 00:09:35.336
slowing down, why did you fail?

00:09:35.336 --> 00:09:37.859
It's typically five separate reasons:

00:09:37.859 --> 00:09:40.484
you work with the wrong
people, the idea was bad,

00:09:40.484 --> 00:09:42.973
the timing was wrong,
it wasn't a monopoly,

00:09:42.973 --> 00:09:45.546
the product didn't work, and okay,

00:09:45.546 --> 00:09:47.480
next time I'm working
with different people.

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:48.977
You figure out one of the five things,

00:09:48.977 --> 00:09:51.411
you're likely to fail again.

00:09:51.411 --> 00:09:53.903
So I think, I think
failure is not something

00:09:53.903 --> 00:09:55.728
we can learn very much from.

00:09:55.728 --> 00:09:59.127
I think that's sort of a
myth that I would challenge.

00:09:59.127 --> 00:10:01.010
But what I think you should do

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:03.742
when something goes badly wrong is

00:10:03.742 --> 00:10:07.550
you just keep going; you do something else

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:09.133
and you don't dwell on the past.

00:10:09.133 --> 00:10:11.696
Already in the time of
Shakespeare the word ape

00:10:11.696 --> 00:10:14.122
meant both primate and to imitate

00:10:14.122 --> 00:10:16.309
and there is something
very deep in human nature

00:10:16.309 --> 00:10:19.150
that is imitated; it's how,
it has a lot of good things,

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:20.942
it's how language gets learned by kids.

00:10:20.942 --> 00:10:23.864
It's how culture gets
transmitted in our society.

00:10:23.864 --> 00:10:27.242
But it also can lead to sort
of a lot of insane behavior.

00:10:27.242 --> 00:10:29.421
It can lead to the madness
of crowds, to bubbles,

00:10:29.421 --> 00:10:32.499
to sort of mass delusions
of one sort or another

00:10:32.499 --> 00:10:36.249
and I think it can, I
think it's advertising.

00:10:37.603 --> 00:10:39.252
We always think of, we
always tell ourselves

00:10:39.252 --> 00:10:41.035
that we're not that prone to this

00:10:41.035 --> 00:10:42.788
and I think that's something
I would encourage all of us

00:10:42.788 --> 00:10:44.808
to rethink; you know, we always think of

00:10:44.808 --> 00:10:47.473
advertising as something that
just afflicts other people,

00:10:47.473 --> 00:10:49.609
that never afflicts ourselves.

00:10:49.609 --> 00:10:51.922
I think this is very far
from the case and so,

00:10:51.922 --> 00:10:54.879
and so, the monopoly
competition is not just this

00:10:54.879 --> 00:10:58.306
intellectual failure,
it's also this thing where

00:10:58.306 --> 00:11:02.248
you have a tiny door where
everyone's trying to rush through

00:11:02.248 --> 00:11:05.197
and there may be around the corner a vast

00:11:05.197 --> 00:11:07.349
and a secret gate that no ones taking

00:11:07.349 --> 00:11:09.873
and you should always find the secret path

00:11:09.873 --> 00:11:12.217
and go ahead and take that.

00:11:12.217 --> 00:11:13.595
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:11:13.595 --> 00:11:16.011
- Now we made this video
because Chain Wan Chung

00:11:16.011 --> 00:11:17.978
on YouTube asked Evan to make it.

00:11:17.978 --> 00:11:19.115
If you have a famous entrepreneur

00:11:19.115 --> 00:11:21.762
that you really really really
want Evan to make a video on

00:11:21.762 --> 00:11:23.375
leave it in the comments below.

00:11:23.375 --> 00:11:26.148
- I'd also love to know which
of Peter Thiel's top ten rules

00:11:26.148 --> 00:11:28.214
hit you the most; leave
it in the comments.

00:11:28.214 --> 00:11:29.459
We're going to join in the discussion.

00:11:29.459 --> 00:11:30.519
Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:30.519 --> 00:11:32.661
Continue to believe
and we'll see you soon.

00:11:32.661 --> 00:11:34.994
(swooshing)

